The issue in the JavaScript code is that it uses `var` to declare the loop counter `i`. This makes `i` have function scope instead of block scope. When the `setTimeout` function runs, the loop has already completed, so `i` is 3 for all iterations when the console log executes.

To fix this issue, use `let` instead of `var`; `let` is block-scoped, so each iteration of the loop will maintain its own value of `i`.

Here's the corrected code:

```javascript
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 0);
}
```

Now, the output will correctly print `0`, `1`, and `2`.

